  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}]}^{\u2032}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
